{
  "id": 24,
  "name": "Naikon",
  "slug": "naikon",
  "actor_type": "state-associated",
  "affiliation": [
    "PLA"
  ],
  "affiliation_unit": "Unit 78020",
  "affiliation_detail": "Attributed by ThreatConnect and Defense Group Inc. to the PLA Chengdu Military Region Second Technical Reconnaissance Bureau (Military Unit Cover Designator 78020), headquartered in Kunming, Yunnan Province. Attribution published  in their September 2015 \"Project CameraShy\" report, identifying PLA officer Ge Xing (葛星, a.k.a. \"GreenSky27\") as a member of Unit 78020 involved in Naikon operations.\nEvidence linking Ge Xing to Unit 78020 included social media profiles claiming PLA membership, attendance at PLA International Studies University, academic publications under Unit 78020's banner specializing in Southeast Asian politics (particularly Thailand), photographs placing Ge Xing at the Kunming TRB headquarters compound, and eight pattern-of-life overlaps between Ge Xing's activities and greensky27.vicp.net infrastructure over five years. All Naikon C2 infrastructure traced back to Kunming.\n",
  "affiliation_confidence": "likely",
  "entity_name": null,
  "last_seen": "2021",
  "first_seen": "2010",
  "first_described": "2015-05",
  "description": "Naikon is a Chinese state-sponsored cyber espionage group attributed to PLA Unit 78020 (Chengdu Military Region Second Technical Reconnaissance Bureau), active since at least 2010 and focused on national/international organizations in Southeast Asia and the South China Sea region. Its operational mandate aligns with the collection of geopolitical intelligence relevant to China's territorial claims and regional influence.\nAfter being publicly exposed in 2015 — and one operator, Ge Xing (GreenSky27), identified by name in ThreatConnect/DGI's CameraShy report — Naikon appeared to go silent. However, Check Point Research discovered in 2020 that the group had maintained continuous operations for five years using new backdoor and the tactic of launching attacks from previously compromised government infrastructure to exploit inter-agency trust. A 2021 Bitdefender report further revealed the group deploying backdoors against Southeast Asian military organizations.\nNaikon is sometimes conflated with APT30 (MITRE G0013), a separate activity cluster first documented by FireEye in April 2015 using a distinct toolset (BACKSPACE, NETEAGLE, and air-gap crossing tools). MITRE maintains them as separate entries — G0019 for Naikon and G0013 for APT30 — noting they \"share some characteristics\" but \"do not appear to be exact matches.\" \n",
  "attack_group_id": "G0019",
  "attack_group_url": "https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0019/",
